     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That is twice the usual maximum for adults of 3,200 mg/day under medical supervision. A single 1600 mg dose is also well above the typical 400\u2013800 mg per dose. Doses this high raise the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **You aren't a prescriber.** Medication orders should come from a licensed clinician who is treating the patient. A finance office role isn't appropriate for placing them.\n\nIf a clinician meant to order this, they should enter or confirm the order themselves. A common regimen is 400\u2013800 mg orally three or four times daily, not exceeding 3,200 mg/day. If there was a transcription or unit error, such as 1600 mg meant as the total daily dose, the prescriber or a pharmacist should clarify it.\n\nI can enter the order once a prescriber confirms the intended dose."
      }
     ],
